  <div id="calc-bodyfat-explain" class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">How We Calculate This</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        This uses the <strong>U.S. Navy circumference method</strong>, developed by the Naval Health Research
        Center from a few tape-measure inputs (height, neck, waist, and — for women — hip) instead of
        specialized equipment. The formula relies on <strong>base-10 logarithms</strong> (log₁₀), not natural
        log — mixing the two up is the most common manual calculation error.
        <br /><br />
        In the original validation against underwater (hydrostatic) weighing, it correlated at roughly r=0.90
        for men and r=0.85 for women, with a typical error margin of about 3–4 percentage points — a solid
        estimate, not a clinical-grade measurement. It's also less reliable at the extremes of body composition,
        e.g. very high muscle mass or very low body fat, where circumference alone tracks fat mass less well.
        <br /><br />
        Where you place the tape matters more than most people expect. Measure your <strong>neck</strong> just
        below the larynx (Adam's apple), and your <strong>waist</strong> at the navel — the same spot in both
        cases regardless of where your pants sit. For the hip measurement (women only), measure at the widest
        point around the hips and glutes. The tape should sit snug against the skin, not compressing it — pulled
        too tight, it reads smaller than your true circumference and understates your result. Because the
        formula is sensitive to exact placement, the most useful thing you can do isn't a single perfect
        reading — it's measuring the same spot, the same way, every time. That consistency is what makes the
        trend over weeks meaningful, even if any one reading is off by a bit.
      </p>
      <strong>Source:</strong> Hodgdon, J.A., &amp; Beckett, M.B. (1984). <em>Prediction of Percent Body Fat for
      U.S. Navy Men and Women from Body Circumferences and Height.</em> Naval Health Research Center Technical
      Reports 84-11 (men) and 84-29 (women).
      <br /><br />
      <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK235939/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the validation data →</a>
    </div>
  </div>

